1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,128
Today's big AI models

2
00:00:02,128 --> 00:00:03,728
have billions of knobs

3
00:00:03,728 --> 00:00:05,620
some hundreds of billions.

4
00:00:05,970 --> 00:00:07,195
Every step of training

5
00:00:07,195 --> 00:00:08,643
you need to know which way

6
00:00:08,643 --> 00:00:09,813
each knob should turn

7
00:00:09,813 --> 00:00:10,481
and how far.

8
00:00:10,831 --> 00:00:12,330
You'd never finish testing

9
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:13,426
them one at a time.

10
00:00:13,776 --> 00:00:14,709
The real trick?

11
00:00:14,709 --> 00:00:16,139
One trip backward tells

12
00:00:16,139 --> 00:00:17,817
you how to turn every knob.

13
00:00:17,817 --> 00:00:19,558
It's called backpropagation.

14
00:00:25,267 --> 00:00:27,162
Last time we saw that a neural

15
00:00:27,162 --> 00:00:29,183
network is layers of small units

16
00:00:29,183 --> 00:00:30,699
each with its own knobs.

17
00:00:31,049 --> 00:00:33,976
This time, we'll build a tiny network and watch

18
00:00:33,976 --> 00:00:36,031
the error run backward through it

19
00:00:36,031 --> 00:00:36,840
knob by knob.

20
00:00:40,967 --> 00:00:42,976
In training, the model makes a guess

21
00:00:42,976 --> 00:00:44,928
we compare it with the right answer

22
00:00:44,928 --> 00:00:46,323
and the gap is the error.

23
00:00:46,673 --> 00:00:49,025
The loss is a score for how wrong it is.

24
00:00:49,025 --> 00:00:50,730
Training is turning the knobs

25
00:00:50,730 --> 00:00:52,141
to push that score down.

26
00:00:52,491 --> 00:00:53,878
Each knob's slope tells

27
00:00:53,878 --> 00:00:55,144
you which way to turn

28
00:00:55,144 --> 00:00:55,867
and how far.

29
00:00:55,867 --> 00:00:58,339
All the slopes together are the gradient.

30
00:00:58,689 --> 00:00:59,845
The obvious way?

31
00:00:59,845 --> 00:01:02,158
Nudge one knob, rerun everything

32
00:01:02,158 --> 00:01:04,832
and see if the error went up or down.

33
00:01:05,182 --> 00:01:07,078
One full rerun per knob.

34
00:01:07,078 --> 00:01:10,079
Billions of knobs, billions of reruns.

35
00:01:10,079 --> 00:01:11,501
For a single step.

36
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:18,612
Backpropagation starts at the answer instead.

37
00:01:18,612 --> 00:01:20,201
Picture a chain of gears

38
00:01:20,201 --> 00:01:22,253
with a pointer on the last one.

39
00:01:22,602 --> 00:01:24,525
Turn the first gear one notch

40
00:01:24,525 --> 00:01:26,051
and the second turns 2.

41
00:01:26,051 --> 00:01:27,709
Turn the second one notch

42
00:01:27,709 --> 00:01:29,168
and the third turns 3.

43
00:01:29,518 --> 00:01:32,207
So how far does the pointer move?

44
00:01:32,207 --> 00:01:34,081
Multiply along the way:

45
00:01:34,081 --> 00:01:35,466
2 × 3. 6 notches.

46
00:01:35,816 --> 00:01:38,237
So start at the pointer and work backward

47
00:01:38,237 --> 00:01:39,359
multiplying by each

48
00:01:39,359 --> 00:01:40,717
gear's ratio as you go.

49
00:01:41,067 --> 00:01:43,001
At each gear you multiply once

50
00:01:43,001 --> 00:01:45,064
and reuse what you already have.

51
00:01:45,064 --> 00:01:46,353
Nothing gets redone.

52
00:01:46,703 --> 00:01:48,878
A neural network is a chain like this.

53
00:01:48,878 --> 00:01:50,481
Each knob affects the answer

54
00:01:50,481 --> 00:01:52,084
through the layers after it.

55
00:01:52,084 --> 00:01:54,374
So the error can flow back the same way.

56
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:00,681
Here's a real, tiny network:

57
00:02:00,681 --> 00:02:02,239
two layers, 7 knobs.

58
00:02:02,239 --> 00:02:03,952
It guesses taxi fares.

59
00:02:03,952 --> 00:02:06,289
We give it a 5-kilometer ride.

60
00:02:06,639 --> 00:02:09,580
In the first layer, two units, A and B

61
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:11,593
use two knobs each to turn

62
00:02:11,593 --> 00:02:13,605
the five into a 6 and a 4.

63
00:02:13,955 --> 00:02:15,177
The second layer

64
00:02:15,177 --> 00:02:17,086
multiplies each by a knob

65
00:02:17,086 --> 00:02:20,294
adds a seventh knob, and guesses: 29 yuan.

66
00:02:20,644 --> 00:02:22,417
The real fare was 23.

67
00:02:22,417 --> 00:02:23,346
6 too high.

68
00:02:23,346 --> 00:02:26,301
That six is the error we pass back.

69
00:02:26,651 --> 00:02:27,818
Last layer first.

70
00:02:27,818 --> 00:02:29,672
A knob's blame is the error

71
00:02:29,672 --> 00:02:31,663
times the number fed into it.

72
00:02:31,663 --> 00:02:32,830
That's its slope.

73
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:34,955
So: 6 × 6, 36.

74
00:02:34,955 --> 00:02:36,222
6 × 4, 24.

75
00:02:36,222 --> 00:02:41,293
And the seventh knob's input is 1, so 6.

76
00:02:41,293 --> 00:02:44,715
Bigger input, bigger blame.

77
00:02:45,065 --> 00:02:45,904
One layer back

78
00:02:45,904 --> 00:02:48,303
the error flows along the wires and gets

79
00:02:48,303 --> 00:02:50,341
multiplied by each knob it passes.

80
00:02:50,691 --> 00:02:54,585
For A, the error times 2: 12.

81
00:02:54,585 --> 00:02:57,002
For B, times 1: 6.

82
00:02:57,352 --> 00:02:59,135
For their own two knobs

83
00:02:59,135 --> 00:03:01,384
it's error times input again.

84
00:03:01,734 --> 00:03:02,583
One trip back

85
00:03:02,583 --> 00:03:04,805
and all seven knobs have a number.

86
00:03:04,805 --> 00:03:07,288
We guessed high, so they all turn down

87
00:03:07,288 --> 00:03:08,725
some more than others.

88
00:03:09,075 --> 00:03:10,838
We turn each knob down

89
00:03:10,838 --> 00:03:12,841
a little and guess again.

90
00:03:12,841 --> 00:03:15,486
29 becomes about 24. Much closer.

91
00:03:15,836 --> 00:03:17,872
We checked the slow way too:

92
00:03:17,872 --> 00:03:19,908
one run with nothing changed

93
00:03:19,908 --> 00:03:22,308
then one per nudged knob. 8 runs.

94
00:03:22,658 --> 00:03:24,533
Same seven numbers, exactly.

95
00:03:24,533 --> 00:03:26,608
Backpropagation needed just one

96
00:03:26,608 --> 00:03:28,617
run forward and one trip back.

97
00:03:28,967 --> 00:03:31,052
Scale up to a billion knobs

98
00:03:31,052 --> 00:03:34,063
and the slow way is 1,000,000,001 runs.

99
00:03:34,413 --> 00:03:36,211
Even at one second a run

100
00:03:36,211 --> 00:03:37,783
that's over 30 years.

101
00:03:37,783 --> 00:03:38,757
For one step.

102
00:03:39,107 --> 00:03:40,862
Backpropagation is still

103
00:03:40,862 --> 00:03:42,177
one trip each way.

104
00:03:42,177 --> 00:03:44,663
The trip back costs about two runs

105
00:03:44,663 --> 00:03:46,125
so roughly 3 in all.

106
00:03:46,475 --> 00:03:48,994
Without it, nobody could train these models.

107
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:55,270
So where does this come from?

108
00:03:55,270 --> 00:03:56,617
In 1986, Rumelhart

109
00:03:56,617 --> 00:03:58,414
Hinton and Williams laid

110
00:03:58,414 --> 00:04:00,584
it out in the journal Nature.

111
00:04:00,934 --> 00:04:02,295
They found each knob's

112
00:04:02,295 --> 00:04:03,532
slope in two passes:

113
00:04:03,532 --> 00:04:05,697
forward, then back from the output.

114
00:04:06,047 --> 00:04:07,603
They also found that the units

115
00:04:07,603 --> 00:04:09,160
in the middle learn to pick up

116
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:10,664
useful patterns on their own.

117
00:04:11,014 --> 00:04:12,894
Others had similar ideas earlier.

118
00:04:12,894 --> 00:04:14,945
This paper showed the method working

119
00:04:14,945 --> 00:04:16,768
on networks with several layers.

120
00:04:17,118 --> 00:04:18,458
Today, in PyTorch

121
00:04:18,458 --> 00:04:21,139
a popular tool for building models

122
00:04:21,139 --> 00:04:24,214
a single line does it: loss.backward().

123
00:04:24,214 --> 00:04:26,264
Every knob gets its slope.

124
00:04:26,614 --> 00:04:28,857
That's automatic differentiation.

125
00:04:28,857 --> 00:04:31,101
Underneath, it's backpropagation.

126
00:04:32,633 --> 00:04:34,374
Backpropagation is passing

127
00:04:34,374 --> 00:04:35,579
the blame backward

128
00:04:35,579 --> 00:04:37,722
from the output, layer by layer.

129
00:04:38,072 --> 00:04:38,922
One trip back

130
00:04:38,922 --> 00:04:41,407
and every knob knows which way to turn

131
00:04:41,407 --> 00:04:42,192
and how far.

132
00:04:42,542 --> 00:04:43,817
But with so many knobs

133
00:04:43,817 --> 00:04:45,730
a model can memorize the practice

134
00:04:45,730 --> 00:04:47,643
questions and get new ones wrong.

135
00:04:47,993 --> 00:04:50,077
That's overfitting, and it's up next.
